---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import NewsletterSignup from '../../components/NewsletterSignup.astro';
import SourcesStandards from '../../components/SourcesStandards.astro';
import { reviewedByJabin } from '../../lib/person.js';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is a drillpipe pressure schedule and why do I need one?',
    a: 'It is the standpipe pressure to hold at each stage of pumping kill mud down the string, stepping from ICP down to FCP as heavier mud replaces the original mud in the drill string. Holding a schedule rather than jumping straight to FCP keeps bottomhole pressure constant while the density inside the pipe is changing — skip it and you either underbalance or overbalance the well partway through the kill.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the schedule step down in strokes, not time?',
    a: 'Because the variable that matters is how much of the string has been displaced by kill mud, and that tracks pump strokes directly, not the clock. Pump rate changes, but the volume per stroke does not, so strokes-to-bit is the reliable reference for where the kill-weight mud front actually is.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do I need both drill string and annular volume?',
    a: 'Strokes to bit uses only the drill string capacity, because that is what has to be displaced before kill mud reaches bit. Bottoms-up strokes uses annular volume, because that is what has to be pumped afterward to bring the original mud and any influx back to surface. Total circulation is both together — the full round trip.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if I get pump output wrong?',
    a: 'Every strokes-based number on this page scales directly off it — strokes to bit, bottoms-up, total circulation, and every step in the pressure schedule. A pump output that is off by 5% shifts the whole schedule by roughly the same amount, which is why measured pump output beats a nameplate figure.',
  },
];

const webAppLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/kill-sheet#app",
  "name": "Kill Sheet Calculator: KMW, ICP, FCP & Pressure Schedule",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/kill-sheet",
  "description": "A kill sheet is the worksheet a rig crew fills out after shutting in on a kick to calculate kill mud weight, ICP, FCP, strokes to bit, bottoms-up, and the full drillpipe pressure step-down schedule to displace kill mud.",
  "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
  "applicationSubCategory": "Drilling engineering calculator",
  "operatingSystem": "Any (web browser)",
  "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isAccessibleForFree": true,
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#organization" }
};

// reviewedBy belongs on WebPage per schema.org, not on WebApplication — see
// src/lib/person.js. A separate WebPage node carries it instead of
// WebApplication itself.
const webPageLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/kill-sheet#webpage",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/kill-sheet",
  "reviewedBy": reviewedByJabin()
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Kill Sheet Calculator: KMW, ICP, FCP & Pressure Schedule"
  description="A kill sheet is the worksheet a rig crew fills out after shutting in on a kick to calculate kill mud weight, ICP, FCP, strokes to bit, and bottoms-up."
  extraJsonLd={[webAppLd, webPageLd]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' }, { label: 'Kill Sheet', href: '/calculators/kill-sheet' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/calculators/kill-sheet"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/calculators/kill-sheet" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Calculator</div>
    <h1>Kill Sheet <span>Calculator</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub"><strong>A kill sheet</strong> is the worksheet a rig crew fills out after shutting in on a kick to work out kill mud weight, circulating pressures, and the drillpipe pressure step-down schedule needed to safely regain control of the well. Use this after shutting in on a kick to calculate kill mud weight, circulating pressures, strokes, and the full drillpipe pressure step-down schedule — the same core math taught in IWCF and IADC well control certification.</p>
    <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);margin-top:8px;">Reviewed for accuracy by <a href="/about/jabin-travis" style="color:var(--amber);">Jabin Travis</a>, IADC WellSharp-certified driller.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="calc-box">
          <div class="calc-title">🚨 Well &amp; Kick Data</div>
          <p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--fog);margin-bottom:20px;line-height:1.65;">Requires the well to already be shut in with SIDPP recorded. See the <a href="/drilling/well-control" style="color:var(--amber);">shut-in procedure</a> if you haven't done this yet.</p>

          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ks-tvd">True Vertical Depth (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ks-tvd" value="10000">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="omw">Original Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
              <input type="number" id="omw" value="10.0" step="0.1">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="sidpp">SIDPP (psi)</label>
              <input type="number" id="sidpp" value="300">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="spr">Slow Circ. Pressure @ SPM (psi)</label>
              <input type="number" id="spr" value="450">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="sicp">SICP (psi)</label>
              <input type="number" id="sicp" value="280">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="maasp">MAASP (psi)</label>
              <input type="number" id="maasp" value="1200">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-title" style="margin-top:24px;">⚙ Pump Data</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="pumpout">Pump Output (bbl/stroke)</label>
              <input type="number" id="pumpout" value="0.1" step="0.001">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-title" style="margin-top:24px;">🔩 Drill String Volume</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="dp-len">Drill Pipe Length (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="dp-len" value="9500">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="dp-cap">Drill Pipe Capacity (bbl/ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="dp-cap" value="0.01776" step="0.00001">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="bha-len">Collars/BHA Length (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="bha-len" value="500">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="bha-cap">Collars/BHA Capacity (bbl/ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="bha-cap" value="0.0087" step="0.00001">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-title" style="margin-top:24px;">⭕ Annular Volume</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="dpann-len">DP/Open Hole Annulus Length (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="dpann-len" value="9500">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="dpann-cap">DP/Open Hole Annulus Capacity (bbl/ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="dpann-cap" value="0.1215" step="0.0001">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="bhaann-len">Collars/Open Hole Annulus Length (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="bhaann-len" value="500">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="bhaann-cap">Collars/Open Hole Annulus Capacity (bbl/ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="bhaann-cap" value="0.0836" step="0.0001">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-output visible" aria-live="polite" id="kill-output" style="margin-top:20px;">
            <div class="output-line headline"><span class="output-label">Kill Mud Weight</span><span class="output-value" id="out-kmw">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line headline"><span class="output-label">Initial Circulating Pressure (ICP)</span><span class="output-value" id="out-icp">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line headline"><span class="output-label">Final Circulating Pressure (FCP)</span><span class="output-value" id="out-fcp">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Total Drill String Volume</span><span class="output-value" id="out-stringvol">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Total Annular Volume</span><span class="output-value" id="out-annvol">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Strokes to Bit</span><span class="output-value" id="out-strokesbit">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Bottoms-Up Strokes</span><span class="output-value" id="out-strokesup">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">Total Circulation (Strokes to Kill)</span><span class="output-value" id="out-strokestotal">—</span></div>
          </div>

          <div class="table-wrap mt-24">
            <table id="sched-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Cumulative Strokes</th><th>Drillpipe Pressure</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody></tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="danger-box mt-24" id="maasp-warning" style="max-width:700px;display:none;">
          <strong>⚠ SICP exceeds MAASP.</strong> That's a serious well control problem on its own, before a kill has even started — stop and involve your certified well control supervisor immediately rather than proceeding with a standard kill sheet.
        </div>

        <div class="danger-box mt-24" style="max-width:700px;">
          <strong>This is a reference tool, not an operational authority.</strong> Real well control decisions must be made by your certified well control supervisor following your company's approved procedures. Never act on a kill sheet calculation from a website alone during an actual well control event.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The Formulas Used</h2>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Kill Mud Weight</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>KMW = OMW + (SIDPP ÷ (0.052 × TVD))</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">The mud weight required to exactly balance formation pressure at the bit. Per IADC WellSharp convention, the result is rounded <strong>up</strong> to the nearest 0.1 ppg, and that rounded value — not the raw calculation — is what carries into FCP below.</dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Initial Circulating Pressure</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>ICP = SIDPP + Slow Circ. Pressure</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">The standpipe pressure to hold at the start of the kill circulation.</dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Final Circulating Pressure</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>FCP = Slow Circ. Pressure × (KMW ÷ OMW)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">The standpipe pressure once kill mud has fully displaced the wellbore.</dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Strokes to Bit / Bottoms-Up</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Strokes = Volume (bbl) ÷ Pump Output (bbl/stroke)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Strokes to bit uses drill string volume. Bottoms-up uses annular volume. Total circulation is both added together.</dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Pressure Step Down the String</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Pressure at step i = ICP − ((ICP − FCP) ÷ 10) × i</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Ten even steps from ICP at the start to FCP once kill mud reaches the bit — the schedule you hold the drillpipe pressure to while pumping.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>What Is Slow Circulating Pressure?</h3>
        <p>SCR (sometimes SPR) is the standpipe pressure recorded at a reduced, standardized pump rate — measured routinely during normal operations specifically so it's available if a kick occurs. Without a current SCR reading, ICP and FCP can't be calculated accurately, which is why crews record it every shift as standard practice.</p>

        <p>For the full step-by-step shut-in procedure and the difference between the Driller's Method and Wait-and-Weight, see the <a href="/drilling/well-control">well control reference page</a>. For string and annular capacities on your own pipe sizes, the <a href="/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet">drilling formulas cheat sheet</a> has the capacity formula written out. On a directional or horizontal well, the <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet-horizontal">horizontal kill sheet additions</a> extend this same schedule with two more checkpoints at kick-off point and end of build. Once a kill mud weight is chosen, check it against the shoe on the <a href="/calculators/mamw">MAMW calculator</a>, work out the barite needed to actually reach it on the <a href="/calculators/barite-required">barite required calculator</a>, and re-check pump pressure at the reduced kill rate with the <a href="/calculators/pump-pressure-adjustment">pump pressure adjustment calculator</a> if SCR was recorded at a different stroke rate. For supervisor-level kill method comparison, MPD, and regional kill considerations, see the <a href="/pillars/advanced-well-control-and-hydrostatics">Advanced Well Control guide</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
    <NewsletterSignup lang="en" />
  </section>

  <SourcesStandards lang="en" ids={['api53', 'cfr250g', 'iwcf', 'iadcWellsharp']} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .output-line.headline .output-value { color: var(--amber); font-size: 18px; }
</style>

<script is:inline src="/scripts/calculators/kill-sheet.js" defer></script>
